"about": "Hand labels for words the Etymological Wordnet table lacks or leaves at a Middle English form with no onward link, the 1,200 most frequent such words, plus a second pass of about 330 (the most frequent words still unknown, and frequent table answers that were plainly wrong: homographs such as found, put, lay and mean, French loans the table gives as native such as place, letter and fine, and the title Miss, left out because the verb is native and the title French) over the blind-test shelf (23 period novels, 5 fantasy books, StoryDaemon and Opus scenes). G = Old English or Norse, R = French or Latin, Gk = Greek, O = other, X = not a word to count (names, interjections, dialect spellings, a homograph whose senses differ in origin). Labelled by Claude on 2026-11-14; checked blind against 300 words the table does resolve: 288 agree (96%), the 12 disagreements mostly early Latin loans into Old English (tower, port, scholar) and homographs (sold, bill, rate).",
